prop title = "Posterior Visualizer"
prop date = "2026-09-04"

fn fmt(v, minDecimals) {
  if !Number.isFinite(v) {
    return "∞"
  }
  if v == 0 {
    return "0"
  }
  if Math.abs(v) >= 0.001 {
    return v.toFixed(Math.min(7, Math.max(minDecimals, 2 - Math.floor(Math.log10(Math.abs(v))))))
  }
  return v.toPrecision(3)
}

The posterior is crucial in a variety of machine learning methods, such as
variational auto-encoders (VAEs). Here, I seek to build up and demonstrate
a visual understanding of what the posterior actually *is*, and the steps
involved in computing it.

The posterior is defined in Bayes' rule, which relates it to the likelihood,
the prior, and the evidence/marginal probability.

$$p(\theta \mid X) = \frac{p(X \mid \theta)\, p(\theta)}{p(X)}$$

Let's try and see if we can better illustrate what Bayes' rule is actually
relating. We consider a motivating example of a number of coin tosses of a
(potentially) biased coin. We assume that the actual bias is unknown or
unobservable to us, but we can estimate it using a *parameter* $\theta$.

## $\theta$

In this example, we have a single parameter $\theta$ which represents the coin's
*bias*, with $\theta = 0.5$ representing a fair coin, $\theta = 1.0$ representing
a coin which will only ever come up heads, and $\theta = 0.0$ representing a coin
which will only ever come up tails.

var theta = 0.7
<Slider value=$theta min={0} max={1} step={0.01}>$\theta$</Slider>

## $X$

$X$ is our dataset: our actual observed set of coin flips. In the real world, you
don't usually get to choose this, but here you can build your own $X$ below.
Tap on a coin face to flip it.

var flips = ["H", "T", "H", "H"]
<Coins $flips max={10} />
let heads = flips.filter(f => f == "H").length
let tails = flips.length - heads

$h$ = <b class="heads">{heads}</b> {heads == 1 ? "head" : "heads"}, $t$ = <b class="tails">{tails}</b> {tails == 1 ? "tail" : "tails"}

## Likelihood: $p(X \mid \theta)$

Now that we have our observed dataset $X$ and our parameter $\theta$, one of the
first things we might want to know is: how good is our guess? Intuitively, if you
rolled heads 10 times in a row, you would think it's highly unlikely that the coin
was fair, and you would think it's even *more* unlikely that the coin is tails-biased.

Take a second and consider exactly why that is. If you do, you'll probably back
up your claim using some calculation assuming the probability of heads is 0.5, and
then showing how unlikely it is to get 10 heads in a row. And then if we assume the
probability of heads is 0.1 (i.e the coin has a 90% change of tails), it's even
*less* likely.

This notion is precisely what **likelihood** quantifies. It quantifies "*given*
our guess of $\theta$, how *likely* is our dataset $X$?" Below you can see the math
worked out for your current guess of $\theta = {theta}$.

let likelihood = x => x ** heads * (1 - x) ** tails
<CoinLikelihood flips={flips} theta={theta} />

Once we have the likelihood for one value of $\theta$, the natural next step is
to take all possible values of $\theta$ and plot the likelihood *function*.

<Plot curves={[{f: likelihood, color: "var(--red)", area: true}]} theta={theta} />
<ul class="legend">
  <li class="like" class="hidden" if flips.length == 0>likelihood</li>
</ul>
<Slider value=$theta min={0} max={1} step={0.01}>$\theta$</Slider>

This lets us see which values of $\theta$ best explain the dataset. Look at your
value of $\theta$ plotted above using the vertical line. Is it the most likely
value? Which other values may explain $X$ better?

## Prior: $p(\theta)$

So we have our parameter $\theta$ which we can vary and see which values best fit
the data. But what if, just before the coin tosses, a mysterious stranger pulled
you aside and said "don't ask me how I know, but the coin is biased 70% heads."
This would make you much likely to at least start your guess off at 0.7. 
Conversely, you may have no clue about the coin, but generally believe like most
of us that most coins are fair coins, in which case perhaps you would start your
guess at 0.5, and be much more doubtful as the coin got more biased either way.

We want some ability to nudge our algorithm to take into account this prior
information we may have about the world. Fittingly, this is called the **prior**
in probability, and written $p(\theta)$.

Below, you can experiment with different kinds of distributions for your
prior using a Beta distribution. You don't really need to know what the means.
Just know that it's bound from 0 to 1, which makes it a natural fit for
$\theta$ here. It also allows us to easily express both a uniform distribution
($\alpha = \beta = 1$) and a bell-shaped distribution if you increase
$\alpha$ and $\beta$ simultaneously. Increasing $\alpha$ pushes the distribution
towards heads at a higher certainty, while increasing $\beta$ pushes the distribution
towards tails.

var alpha = 1
var beta = 1
<Slider value=$alpha min={1} max={10} step={0.1} digits={1}>$\alpha$</Slider>
<Slider value=$beta min={1} max={10} step={0.1} digits={1}>$\beta$</Slider>

fn logGamma(x) {
  if x < 0.5 {
    return Math.log(Math.PI / Math.sin(Math.PI * x)) - logGamma(1 - x)
  }
  let c = [676.5203681218851, -1259.1392167224028, 771.32342877765313, -176.61502916214059,
    12.507343278686905, -0.13857109526572012, 0.0000099843695780195716, 0.00000015056327351493116]
  let y = x - 1
  let t = y + 7.5
  let a = c.reduce((s, ci, i) => s + ci / (y + i + 1), 0.99999999999980993)
  return 0.5 * Math.log(2 * Math.PI) + (y + 0.5) * Math.log(t) - t + Math.log(a)
}
fn logBeta(a, b) = logGamma(a) + logGamma(b) - logGamma(a + b)
fn betaPdf(x, a, b) {
  if x <= 0 || x >= 1 {
    let edge = x <= 0 ? a : b
    return edge < 1 ? 1 / 0 : edge > 1 ? 0 : Math.exp(-logBeta(a, b))
  }
  return Math.exp((a - 1) * Math.log(x) + (b - 1) * Math.log(1 - x) - logBeta(a, b))
}
let prior = x => betaPdf(x, alpha, beta)

<Plot curves={[{f: prior, color: "var(--blue)", area: true}]} floor={1.3} theta={theta} />

Evaluating $p(\theta)$ at $\theta$ gives us the density <b class="prior">{fmt(prior(theta), 3)}</b>

## Evidence: $p(X)$

Whereas the likelihood tells us how well each $\theta$ explains $X$, the evidence
tells us how well our *model* explains $X$, by averaging the likelihood weighted
by the prior.

$$p(X) = \int p(X \mid \theta)\, p(\theta)\, d \theta$$

We plot the **joint** $p(X \mid \theta)\, p(\theta)$ below. The area
under this curve represents how well our model explains $X$, which is $p(X)$.

let joint = x => likelihood(x) * prior(x)
let evidence = Math.exp(logBeta(alpha + heads, beta + tails) - logBeta(alpha, beta))

<Plot curves={[{f: joint, color: "var(--purple)", area: true}]} theta={theta} />

$p(X) =$ <b>{fmt(evidence, 4)}</b>

## Posterior: $p(\theta \mid X)$

Now we have finally arrived: the posterior. The posterior is the flipside of
the prior. Whereas the prior asks "how likely is $\theta$ *before* seeing
$X$," the posterior asks "how likely is $\theta$ *after* seeing $X$?" It is an
update of the prior, weighted by the likelihood.

$$p(\theta \mid X) = \frac{p(X \mid \theta) p(\theta)}{p(X)}$$

Notice that this is literally just Bayes' rule. Below are all parameter sliders
so you can directly see the effects of different $\theta$ and the prior $p(\theta)$
on the posterior.

let posterior = x => betaPdf(x, alpha + heads, beta + tails)

<Slider value=$theta min={0} max={1} step={0.01}>$\theta$</Slider>
<Slider value=$alpha min={1} max={10} step={0.1} digits={1}>$\alpha$</Slider>
<Slider value=$beta min={1} max={10} step={0.1} digits={1}>$\beta$</Slider>

<Plot curves={[
  {f: prior, color: "var(--blue)"},
  {f: likelihood, color: "var(--red)", dashed: true, rescale: true, hidden: flips.length == 0},
  {f: posterior, color: "var(--purple)", area: true}
]} theta={theta} />
<ul class="legend">
  <li class="prior">prior</li>
  <li class="like" class="hidden" if flips.length == 0>likelihood (rescaled)</li>
  <li class="post">posterior</li>
</ul>

<p class="payoff">$p(\theta \mid X)$ = <span class="frac"><span class="num"><b class="like">{fmt(likelihood(theta), 4)}</b> <span class="op">×</span> <b class="prior">{fmt(prior(theta), 3)}</b></span><span class="den">{fmt(evidence, 4)}</span></span> = <b class="post">{fmt(posterior(theta), 3)}</b></p>

Notice a few consequences. First, if we use the uniform distribution, we are
essentially saying that we don't know anything about $\theta$. Consequently,
we are pulled 100% to likelihood, because there *is* no prior information to
update. Looking at the math, you can see that $p(\theta) = 1$ in the case of
uniform, and thus

$$p(\theta \mid X) = \frac{p(X \mid \theta) \cdot 1}{p(X)} = \frac{p(X \mid \theta)}{p(X)}$$

just a scaled version of the likelihood.

Also, notice that the *more* you increase $\alpha$ or $\beta$, we are expressing
*more* certainty over our prior, and our posterior is pulled closer to the prior.
Conversely, decreasing $\alpha$ or $\beta$ expresses *less* certainty, and the
posterior is pulled closer to the likelihood.

<style>
.heads, .like { color: var(--red); }
.tails, .prior { color: var(--blue); }
.post { color: var(--purple); }
.hidden { display: none; }
